# Chapter 5 · The Waves Compared: Principles, Goals, Strategies and Effects

> The 2025 question asked: "Discuss the **key principles, goals and strategies** of the different waves of feminism. How have these waves **affected women's lives**?" This chapter puts the four waves side by side under exactly those headings, and adds continuity, change and a verdict.

## 5.1  The master table

::: table | The four waves compared.
@widths 2.4,2.9,2.9,2.9,2.9
Aspect | First (c. 1848–1920) | Second (1960s–80s) | Third (1990s–2000s) | Fourth (c. 2012–)
Key question | Are women citizens? | Why are women still unequal? | Which women? | Why is harassment everywhere?
Principles | Equal natural rights; reason | Personal is political; patriarchy | Diversity; intersectionality; choice | Consent; intersectionality; accountability
Goals | Vote, property, education | Pay, jobs, reproduction, violence | Inclusion, sexuality, identity, global rights | End harassment and violence; safe spaces
Strategies | Petitions, conventions, militancy | Lobbying, lawsuits, consciousness-raising | Culture, zines, music, networks | Hashtags, online testimony, mass marches
Symbols | Seneca Falls 1848; 19th Amendment 1920 | Friedan 1963; Roe 1973 | Walker 1992; Riot Grrrl | #MeToo 2017; Aurat March 2018
Theory | Liberal | Liberal, radical, socialist | Black, postmodern, queer, postcolonial | Intersectional
Main gain | Legal personhood | Rights at work and over the body | Plural, inclusive feminism | Visibility and accountability
Main blind spot | Race and class | Race, sexuality, global South | Individualism, consumerism | Digital divide, online abuse
:::

## 5.2  Principles — how the idea of equality deepened

::: fig pyramid | How each wave deepened the meaning of equality.
First wave: equality before the law — women as citizens
Second wave: equality in work, family and body — the personal is political
Third wave: equality among women — diversity and intersectionality
Fourth wave: equality in everyday life — safety, consent and accountability
:::

Each wave kept the gains of the one before and pushed further: from the **public sphere** (vote, law) into the **private sphere** (family, body), then to **differences among women**, and finally to **everyday experience**, online and offline.

## 5.3  Goals — from one demand to many

- The **first wave** had one great goal — the **vote** — which united very different women.
- The **second wave** had several: **equal pay, reproductive rights, an end to violence**.
- The **third wave** had many, sometimes competing, goals.
- The **fourth wave** re-centred one issue — **sexual harassment and violence** — but framed it intersectionally.

## 5.4  Strategies — how the methods changed

::: fig spectrum | The main method of each wave, from institutional to cultural and digital.
@left Working through institutions
@right Working through culture and networks
10: First wave — petitions, lobbying, conventions
35: Second wave — lawsuits and consciousness-raising
68: Third wave — music, zines, media
92: Fourth wave — hashtags and viral testimony
:::

The **means of communication** shaped each wave: pamphlets and conventions (first); paperback books, magazines and television (second); independent music and the early internet (third); smartphones and social media (fourth).

## 5.4a  The theory behind each wave

Each wave drew mainly on the schools taught in Topic 2:

- The **first wave** was **liberal**: Wollstonecraft and Mill argued that women are rational citizens with equal rights.
- The **second wave** was **liberal** (Friedan, NOW) and **radical and socialist** (Millett, Firestone, Hartmann) — the argument between "reform" and "liberation".
- The **third wave** was **Black, postmodern, queer and postcolonial** (hooks, Crenshaw, Butler, Mohanty).
- The **fourth wave** is **intersectional** and practical: it uses Crenshaw's idea as its starting point and applies it through digital testimony.

## 5.4b  Pakistan through the lens of the waves

::: table | The waves as they appear in Pakistan's history.
@widths 2.4,4.6,7
Wave | Pakistani equivalent | Evidence
First | Welfare and reform (1947–1970) | Vote at independence; APWA (1949); MFLO (1961); girls' schools and colleges
Second | Legal resistance (1980s) | WAF (1981) against the Hudood Ordinances (1979); 12 February 1983; later laws on violence
Third | NGOs and diversity (1990s–2000s) | Beijing (1995); CEDAW (1996); NGO boom; Islamic feminism; rural and minority women
Fourth | Digital and street activism (2018–) | Aurat March; #MeToo; cyber-harassment helplines; bodily autonomy
:::

In Pakistan the phases **overlap** far more than in the West: first-wave goals such as girls' schooling and inheritance are still unfinished while fourth-wave campaigns run online.

## 5.5  How the waves affected women's lives

::: fig mapping | What each wave changed in women's lives.
@left The wave
@right The change in women's lives
First wave: votes and property => Citizens and owners of property
First wave: votes and property => Access to education and professions
Second wave: work, body, violence => Equal pay laws and careers
Second wave: work, body, violence => Control over childbearing
Second wave: work, body, violence => Violence treated as crime
Third wave: diversity => Voice for women of colour, LGBT and Southern women
Fourth wave: digital => Harassment exposed; new workplace rules
:::

1. **Political life.** Women vote and stand for office almost everywhere; by the mid-2020s about a quarter of the world's parliamentarians were women.
2. **Legal status.** Married women own property, sign contracts and seek divorce; discrimination is unlawful in most countries.
3. **Education and work.** Women now outnumber men at university in many countries and work in every profession — though pay gaps and "glass ceilings" remain.
4. **Health and body.** Contraception and, in many countries, safe abortion give women control over childbearing.
5. **Safety.** Rape, domestic violence and harassment are crimes; shelters and hotlines exist.
6. **Voice and identity.** Diverse women speak for themselves; harassment has become costly for perpetrators.

## 5.5a  One life across four waves

Imagine the women of one family across a century and a half.

- A great-great-grandmother born in **1850** in England could not vote, could not keep her wages after marriage, could not attend university and could lose her children in a separation.
- Her granddaughter, born in **1900**, voted for the first time in 1918 or 1928, perhaps worked in a munitions factory in the First World War, and could own property.
- Her granddaughter, born in **1945**, went to university, could get the contraceptive pill, was protected in law against unequal pay, and could leave a violent husband with the help of a shelter.
- Her granddaughter, born in **1990**, grew up with intersectional feminism, could report harassment at work, and used social media to share her experience in 2017.

Each generation inherited the gains of the last — and discovered new problems the last had not solved.

## 5.6  Continuity and change

::: balance | Did the waves break with each other, or continue one struggle?
- **Change:** each wave had new leaders, new issues and new methods, and sometimes criticised the last (the third wave's critique of the second).
- **Continuity:** the same core demand — **equal dignity and freedom for women** — runs through all four. Issues also cross waves: violence was raised in the first wave (temperance and divorce campaigns), and the vote is still defended today.
-> The best answer treats the waves as **stages in one long movement**, each building on the last.
:::

## 5.7  Unfinished business

- The **global gender gap** was only ==69.2%== closed in ==2026==; at the current pace, parity is ==120 years== away (World Economic Forum).
- **Violence:** about **one in three** women worldwide has experienced physical or sexual violence (WHO, ==2021==).
- **Reversals:** the overturning of *Roe v. Wade* (==2022==) shows that gains can be lost.
- **The global South:** in countries like Pakistan (==143rd of 145== in ==2026==), the waves' gains have reached many women only partly.

::: eye | The model structure for 2025
1. Introduction: define a wave; the metaphor (Lear 1968) and its limits. 2–5. One heading per wave, each with principles, goals and strategies as sub-headings. 6. Comparison table. 7. Effects on women's lives — political, legal, economic, bodily, safety, voice. 8. Pakistan. 9. Unfinished business. 10. Conclusion: one long movement. Draw the pyramid or the spectrum of strategies.
:::
